-Generalizes the 2D index-based approach to 3D volumes, handling vertex, edge, and face interactions in three dimensions using stitch cells like pyramids and deformed prisms.
+Generalizes the 2D index-based stitching to 3D volumes, using stitch cells (pyramids, deformed prisms, hexahedra) to connect fine grids to coarse grids. It handles the increased complexity of vertex and edge interactions in 3D space.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of boundary elements, as it uses an index-based approach that leverages the regular structure of the grid boundaries [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the number of stitch cells generated, which is bounded by the perimeter of the fine grid [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Efficient, avoids the computational cost and complexity of constrained Delaunay triangulation, and guarantees crack-free isosurfaces in 3D by preserving quadrilateral boundary faces [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Specific to AMR grids with integer refinement ratios; requires careful handling of mixed refinement states at boundaries [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Strengths:** Index-based approach is efficient as it exploits the regular structure of AMR boundaries. It avoids the insertion of new vertices, which is crucial for dual-grid isosurface extraction [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Limited to 2D grids in this specific method. Requires careful handling of refinement status checks for coarse grid points [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** When extracting crack-free isosurfaces from 2D AMR data using dual grids.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When 3D stitching is required (use 3D AMR Grid Stitching) or when constrained Delaunay triangulation is acceptable and cracks are not a concern (though Delaunay is less efficient for this specific regular structure) [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced in the context of extracting crack-free isosurfaces from Adaptive Mesh Refinement (AMR) data [S1]. It addresses the problem of gaps between dual grids of different hierarchy levels, which would otherwise cause discontinuities in the extracted surface [S1]. The approach was chosen over constrained Delaunay triangulation because the latter requires subdividing quadrilateral boundary faces, which is incompatible with Marching Cubes extraction in 3D [S1].
+The method was introduced in the paper "Extraction of Crack-free Isosurfaces from Adaptive Mesh Refinement Data" [S1]. It addresses the problem of cracks in extracted isosurfaces caused by gaps between AMR grids. The authors chose an index-based approach over constrained Delaunay triangulation because Delaunay requires subdividing quadrilateral boundary faces, which causes cracks in MC-based isosurface extraction [S1].
 
